1
00:00:07,820 --> 00:00:14,960
And Dawn rose from her bed beside lordly Tithonus
to carry light to the immortals and to mortals;

2
00:00:14,960 --> 00:00:21,960
and the gods were sitting down in council, among them
Zeus who thunders on high, whose power is greatest.

3
00:00:21,960 --> 00:00:25,060
And Athena was telling over the many cares of Odysseus,

4
00:00:25,060 --> 00:00:29,920
remembering: it galled her that
he was still in the nymph's house:

5
00:00:29,920 --> 00:00:33,220
"Father Zeus, and you other blessed gods who are forever:

6
00:00:33,220 --> 00:00:39,020
let no sceptered king ever again be willingly kind
and gentle, or know the right way in his heart;

7
00:00:39,020 --> 00:00:44,240
let him rather be harsh always, and act outrage —
since no one remembers godlike Odysseus

8
00:00:44,240 --> 00:00:47,780
among the people he ruled, and he was gentle as a father.

9
00:00:47,780 --> 00:00:53,120
No — he lies on an island suffering strong pains in
the halls of the nymph Calypso, who holds him there

10
00:00:53,120 --> 00:00:59,560
by force; and he cannot come to his own fatherland,
for he has no ships at hand, no oars, no companions

11
00:00:59,560 --> 00:01:02,380
to carry him over the broad back of the sea.

12
00:01:02,380 --> 00:01:08,260
And now, besides, men are bent on killing his beloved
son on his way home: he went for news of his father

13
00:01:08,260 --> 00:01:11,900
to holy Pylos and to shining Lacedaemon."

14
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:14,800
Then Zeus who gathers the clouds answered her:

15
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:18,360
"My child, what a word has
slipped the fence of your teeth!

16
00:01:18,360 --> 00:01:20,760
Was it not you who framed this plan yourself —

17
00:01:20,760 --> 00:01:24,400
that Odysseus should come home
and take vengeance on those men?

18
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:28,260
As for Telemachus: guide him with
your skill — you have the power —

19
00:01:28,260 --> 00:01:33,580
so that he reaches his own fatherland untouched,
and the suitors come back in their ship with nothing done."

20
00:01:33,580 --> 00:01:36,880
He spoke, and turned to Hermes, his dear son:

21
00:01:36,880 --> 00:01:39,680
"Hermes — you are our messenger in all things else:

22
00:01:39,680 --> 00:01:42,880
tell the nymph of the lovely braids our fixed decree:

23
00:01:42,880 --> 00:01:46,380
the homecoming of enduring-hearted
Odysseus — that he shall return

24
00:01:46,380 --> 00:01:49,140
with no escort of gods and none of mortal men:

25
00:01:49,140 --> 00:01:52,600
on a raft bound plank to plank, and suffering pains,

26
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:55,720
on the twentieth day he shall reach rich-soiled Scheria,

27
00:01:55,720 --> 00:01:59,400
the land of the Phaeacians,
who are born close kin to the gods.

28
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:05,260
They will honor him with all their hearts, like a god,
and send him in a ship to his own dear fatherland,

29
00:02:05,260 --> 00:02:10,900
giving him bronze and gold and clothing in plenty,
more than Odysseus would ever have carried out of Troy

30
00:02:10,900 --> 00:02:14,280
had he come home untouched with
his fair share of the spoils.

31
00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:20,720
For so it is his portion: to see his own people and
come to his high-roofed house and his own fatherland."

32
00:02:20,720 --> 00:02:25,760
So he spoke, and the guide,
the slayer of Argus, did not disobey.

33
00:02:25,760 --> 00:02:32,140
At once he bound beneath his feet the beautiful sandals,
ambrosial, golden, that carry him over the water

34
00:02:32,140 --> 00:02:34,760
and over the boundless earth with the breath of the wind;

35
00:02:34,760 --> 00:02:37,340
and he took up the rod with which he charms men's eyes

36
00:02:37,340 --> 00:02:41,900
to sleep, whose eyes he wills,
and wakes others from their slumber.

37
00:02:41,900 --> 00:02:45,300
With this in his hands the strong slayer of Argus flew.

38
00:02:45,300 --> 00:02:49,340
He stepped down on Pieria,
then dropped from the high air to the sea

39
00:02:49,340 --> 00:02:54,240
and skimmed along the waves like a shearwater,
the bird that down the terrible troughs of the barren sea

40
00:02:54,240 --> 00:02:57,080
hunts fish, and wets its beating wings in the brine:

41
00:02:57,080 --> 00:03:00,200
like that bird Hermes rode the crowding waves.

42
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:05,380
But when he reached the island, far away,
he stepped from the violet sea onto dry land

43
00:03:05,380 --> 00:03:08,440
and went on till he came to the great cave where the nymph

44
00:03:08,440 --> 00:03:11,740
of the lovely braids was living; he found her within.

45
00:03:11,740 --> 00:03:18,300
A great fire was burning on the hearth, and the smell
of split cedar and citron-wood burning carried far,

46
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:22,960
all over the island. Inside,
she was singing in a beautiful voice,

47
00:03:22,960 --> 00:03:26,840
moving to and fro at the loom,
weaving with a golden shuttle.

48
00:03:26,840 --> 00:03:29,920
And around the cave a wood grew tall and flourishing:

49
00:03:29,920 --> 00:03:35,840
alder and black poplar and sweet-smelling cypress,
where long-winged birds made their roosts,

50
00:03:35,840 --> 00:03:40,860
little owls and hawks and long-tongued crows
of the sea, whose business is the waters.

51
00:03:40,860 --> 00:03:46,940
And there, trained over the hollow of the cave,
spread a garden vine in its prime, heavy with clusters;

52
00:03:46,940 --> 00:03:53,120
and four springs in a row ran with bright water,
close by one another, turned each its own way;

53
00:03:53,120 --> 00:03:59,700
and around them soft meadows of violet and wild celery
were blooming. There even a deathless one, arriving,

54
00:03:59,700 --> 00:04:03,500
would gaze in wonder at the
sight and be gladdened at heart.

55
00:04:03,500 --> 00:04:07,040
There the guide, the slayer of Argus, stood gazing.

56
00:04:07,040 --> 00:04:12,780
And when his heart had wondered at it all,
he went straight into the wide cave. Nor did Calypso,

57
00:04:12,780 --> 00:04:16,200
shining among goddesses, fail to know him face to face:

58
00:04:16,200 --> 00:04:21,900
for the deathless gods are not unknown to one another,
not even one whose dwelling lies far off.

59
00:04:21,900 --> 00:04:25,200
But great-hearted Odysseus he did not find within:

60
00:04:25,200 --> 00:04:31,260
he sat weeping on the shore, where he always sat,
shredding his heart with tears and groans and pains,

61
00:04:31,260 --> 00:04:35,400
and over the barren sea he would gaze,
letting the tears run.

62
00:04:35,400 --> 00:04:41,920
And Calypso, shining among goddesses, questioned Hermes,
seating him on a chair all bright and gleaming:

63
00:04:41,920 --> 00:04:45,000
"Why have you come to me, Hermes of the golden rod,

64
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:48,260
honored guest and dear?
You have not been used to come before.

65
00:04:48,260 --> 00:04:53,720
Say what is in your mind: my heart bids me do it
if I can do it — if it is a thing that can be done.

66
00:04:53,720 --> 00:04:57,260
But follow me in first, and let me set out guest-fare."

67
00:04:57,260 --> 00:05:03,120
So speaking the goddess drew up a table for him,
heaping it with ambrosia, and mixed the red nectar;

68
00:05:03,120 --> 00:05:06,280
and the guide, the slayer of Argus, drank and ate.

69
00:05:06,280 --> 00:05:11,820
And when he had supped, and steadied his heart with food,
then he spoke out in answer to her question:

70
00:05:11,820 --> 00:05:17,580
"You ask me why I come — goddess to god; and I will
tell you the whole of it truly, since you bid me.

71
00:05:17,580 --> 00:05:20,680
Zeus ordered me here. I did not want to come:

72
00:05:20,680 --> 00:05:24,000
who would choose to cross so vast a stretch of salt water,

73
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:29,960
past speaking? And no city of mortals near,
to offer the gods their victims and their choice hecatombs.

74
00:05:29,960 --> 00:05:35,620
But there is no way for another god to slip past
or thwart the mind of Zeus who bears the aegis.

75
00:05:35,620 --> 00:05:38,600
He says there is a man with you —
the most miserable of all

76
00:05:38,600 --> 00:05:44,480
the men who fought nine years around the city of Priam
and in the tenth year sacked it, and turned for home.

77
00:05:44,480 --> 00:05:51,000
But on the way home they sinned against Athena,
and she roused an evil wind against them, and long seas.

78
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:56,260
There all the rest of his brave companions
perished, but wind and wave carried him here.

79
00:05:56,260 --> 00:05:59,160
That man Zeus bids you send on his way — and quickly:

80
00:05:59,160 --> 00:06:02,800
it is not his portion to die here,
far from his own people;

81
00:06:02,800 --> 00:06:08,700
it is still his portion to see his own people and
come to his high-roofed house and his own fatherland."

82
00:06:08,700 --> 00:06:12,640
So he spoke; and Calypso,
shining among goddesses, shivered,

83
00:06:12,640 --> 00:06:15,500
and lifting her voice she spoke winged words to him:

84
00:06:15,500 --> 00:06:21,220
"You are merciless, you gods — jealous beyond all beings —
who begrudge goddesses their sleeping with men

85
00:06:21,220 --> 00:06:24,860
openly, if one takes a man as her own dear husband.

86
00:06:24,860 --> 00:06:30,460
So when Dawn of the rose-red fingers took Orion,
you gods who live at ease begrudged it to her,

87
00:06:30,460 --> 00:06:36,560
until in Ortygia chaste Artemis of the golden throne
came at him with her gentle arrows and killed him.

88
00:06:36,560 --> 00:06:42,120
So when Demeter of the lovely braids gave way to
her heart and lay with Iasion in love in the bed

89
00:06:42,120 --> 00:06:45,140
of a thrice-plowed fallow field — Zeus was not long

90
00:06:45,140 --> 00:06:48,540
in hearing, and he killed him with
a hurl of the white lightning.

91
00:06:48,540 --> 00:06:52,800
And so now me, you gods:
you begrudge me a mortal man beside me.

92
00:06:52,800 --> 00:06:58,980
I saved him — riding astride his keel, alone,
after Zeus with a hurl of the white lightning had shattered

93
00:06:58,980 --> 00:07:02,240
his fast ship apart in the middle of the wine-dark sea.

94
00:07:02,240 --> 00:07:06,980
There all the rest of his brave companions
perished, but wind and wave carried him here.

95
00:07:06,980 --> 00:07:12,360
And I loved him, and I fed him, and I promised
to make him deathless and unaging all his days.

96
00:07:12,360 --> 00:07:18,540
But since there is no way for another god to slip
past or thwart the mind of Zeus who bears the aegis —

97
00:07:18,540 --> 00:07:24,700
let him go, if that one urges it and commands,
out over the barren sea. Only I will not convey him:

98
00:07:24,700 --> 00:07:32,140
I have no ships at hand, no oars, no companions
to carry him over the broad back of the sea.

99
00:07:32,140 --> 00:07:35,620
But with a willing heart I will
counsel him, and hide nothing,

100
00:07:35,620 --> 00:07:39,320
so that he may reach his own fatherland untouched."

101
00:07:39,320 --> 00:07:42,660
Then the guide, the slayer of Argus, answered her:

102
00:07:42,660 --> 00:07:46,140
"Send him so, then — and beware the wrath of Zeus:

103
00:07:46,140 --> 00:07:50,340
let him not turn against you
afterward and bear hard on you."

104
00:07:50,340 --> 00:07:53,480
So speaking, the strong slayer of Argus went away.

105
00:07:53,480 --> 00:07:59,400
And the queenly nymph went to great-hearted Odysseus,
now that she had heard the message of Zeus.

106
00:07:59,400 --> 00:08:05,780
She found him sitting on the shore. His eyes were
never dry of tears; his sweet life was draining away

107
00:08:05,780 --> 00:08:09,920
in mourning for his homecoming —
the nymph no longer pleased him.

108
00:08:09,920 --> 00:08:13,600
True, at night he would sleep beside her — he had to —

109
00:08:13,600 --> 00:08:17,040
in the hollow caves, unwilling man by willing goddess;

110
00:08:17,040 --> 00:08:20,260
but all his days he sat out on the rocks and the beaches,

111
00:08:20,260 --> 00:08:23,300
shredding his heart with tears and groans and pains,

112
00:08:23,300 --> 00:08:27,380
and over the barren sea he would gaze,
letting the tears run.

113
00:08:27,380 --> 00:08:31,160
Standing near him, she spoke, shining among goddesses:

114
00:08:31,160 --> 00:08:34,480
"Luckless man, do not sit grieving
here any longer; do not let

115
00:08:34,480 --> 00:08:39,460
your life waste away. I am ready now —
wholly — to send you off.

116
00:08:39,460 --> 00:08:44,780
Come: cut long timbers, and join with bronze
a wide raft; and fix an upper half-deck to it,

117
00:08:44,780 --> 00:08:47,940
raised high, to carry you over the misted sea.

118
00:08:47,940 --> 00:08:53,040
And I will put in bread and water and red wine
to your heart's content, to keep hunger from you,

119
00:08:53,040 --> 00:08:55,860
and I will clothe you, and send a fair wind behind you,

120
00:08:55,860 --> 00:09:01,200
so that you may reach your own fatherland untouched —
if the gods are willing, who hold wide heaven,

121
00:09:01,200 --> 00:09:04,380
who are stronger than I to purpose and accomplish."

122
00:09:04,380 --> 00:09:08,340
So she spoke; and much-enduring noble Odysseus shuddered,

123
00:09:08,340 --> 00:09:11,400
and lifting his voice he spoke winged words to her:

124
00:09:11,400 --> 00:09:15,260
"Something else you are plotting here,
goddess — not my sending —

125
00:09:15,260 --> 00:09:18,540
you who bid me cross the great gulf of the sea on a raft,

126
00:09:18,540 --> 00:09:22,180
that terror, that misery: not even the trim fast-running

127
00:09:22,180 --> 00:09:25,620
ships cross it, for all their joy in the wind of Zeus.

128
00:09:25,620 --> 00:09:28,540
I will not set foot on any raft against your will —

129
00:09:28,540 --> 00:09:31,820
not unless you bring yourself,
goddess, to swear me the great oath

130
00:09:31,820 --> 00:09:35,480
that you are not planning some new evil against me."

131
00:09:35,480 --> 00:09:39,300
So he spoke; and Calypso, shining among goddesses, smiled,

132
00:09:39,300 --> 00:09:42,800
and stroked him with her hand,
and spoke a word, and named him:

133
00:09:42,800 --> 00:09:48,300
"You are a rogue indeed — no lightweight in cunning —
to have thought of a speech like that to say to me.

134
00:09:48,300 --> 00:09:54,540
Now let Earth be witness, and the wide Heaven above,
and the down-dripping water of Styx — the greatest oath

135
00:09:54,540 --> 00:10:00,040
and the most terrible the blessed gods can swear —
that I am not planning some new evil against you.

136
00:10:00,040 --> 00:10:06,440
No: I am thinking and advising exactly what I would
devise for my own self, if such need came on me.

137
00:10:06,440 --> 00:10:13,800
My mind is honest through and through, and the heart
in my breast is not made of iron. It knows pity."

138
00:10:13,800 --> 00:10:20,940
So speaking she led the way, shining among goddesses,
swiftly, and he followed in the footsteps of the god.

139
00:10:20,940 --> 00:10:26,640
They came to the hollow cave, the goddess and the man,
and he sat down there on the chair from which Hermes

140
00:10:26,640 --> 00:10:30,160
had risen, and the nymph set out
all manner of food beside him,

141
00:10:30,160 --> 00:10:37,380
to eat and drink, such things as mortal men eat;
and she took her seat facing godlike Odysseus,

142
00:10:37,380 --> 00:10:40,720
and slave-women set ambrosia and nectar beside her.

143
00:10:40,720 --> 00:10:44,040
And their hands went out to the
good things spread before them.

144
00:10:44,040 --> 00:10:50,100
But when they had had their joy of food and drink,
Calypso began, shining among goddesses:

145
00:10:50,100 --> 00:10:54,100
"Zeus-born son of Laertes, Odysseus of the many devices —

146
00:10:54,100 --> 00:10:57,160
so you would go home now, home to your own dear country,

147
00:10:57,160 --> 00:11:02,380
at once, this moment? Go, then —
and joy go with you even so.

148
00:11:02,380 --> 00:11:07,240
But if you knew in your heart how full your measure
of sorrow is before you reach your own country,

149
00:11:07,240 --> 00:11:12,200
you would stay here with me and keep this house,
and be deathless — for all your longing to see

150
00:11:12,200 --> 00:11:15,380
that wife of yours, whom you ache for all your days.

151
00:11:15,380 --> 00:11:21,140
And yet I dare say I am not beneath her —
not in form, not in stature: it is in no way seemly

152
00:11:21,140 --> 00:11:24,920
for mortal women to vie with immortals in body and looks."

153
00:11:24,920 --> 00:11:27,740
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered her:

154
00:11:27,740 --> 00:11:31,600
"Goddess and queen, do not be angry
with me for this. I know it all

155
00:11:31,600 --> 00:11:37,640
myself, well enough: watchful-minded Penelope is
less than you — slighter to look at, smaller —

156
00:11:37,640 --> 00:11:40,680
for she is mortal, and you are deathless and unaging.

157
00:11:40,680 --> 00:11:46,440
But even so I wish — and all my days I long —
to go home, and to look upon my day of homecoming.

158
00:11:46,440 --> 00:11:49,300
And if some god wrecks me again on the wine-dark sea,

159
00:11:49,300 --> 00:11:52,600
I will endure it, with a heart
schooled in enduring sorrows.

160
00:11:52,600 --> 00:11:58,460
Much have I suffered already, much have I labored
through waves and war. Let this be added to the rest."

161
00:11:58,460 --> 00:12:01,920
So he spoke; and the sun went down, and the dusk came on;

162
00:12:01,920 --> 00:12:04,580
and the two of them went into the inner hollow of the cave

163
00:12:04,580 --> 00:12:07,940
and took their joy of love, and stayed side by side.

164
00:12:07,940 --> 00:12:14,220
When Dawn appeared, the early-born, with fingers of rose,
straightway Odysseus put on a cloak and tunic,

165
00:12:14,220 --> 00:12:17,980
and she, the nymph, put on a great silver-shining mantle,

166
00:12:17,980 --> 00:12:21,560
fine-woven and graceful, and cast about her waist a belt,

167
00:12:21,560 --> 00:12:24,980
beautiful, golden, and set a veil on her head above.

168
00:12:24,980 --> 00:12:28,480
Then she turned her mind to the
sending of great-hearted Odysseus.

169
00:12:28,480 --> 00:12:34,440
She gave him a great axe, fitted to his palms,
of bronze, sharpened on both edges, and set in it

170
00:12:34,440 --> 00:12:40,660
a beautiful handle of olive-wood, made firm and fast;
then she gave him a polished adze, and led the way

171
00:12:40,660 --> 00:12:44,260
to the island's farthest edge, where the tall trees grew:

172
00:12:44,260 --> 00:12:47,220
alder and black poplar, and fir that reached the sky,

173
00:12:47,220 --> 00:12:50,760
long dry, dead-seasoned, that would ride for him lightly.

174
00:12:50,760 --> 00:12:53,540
And when she had shown him where the tall trees grew,

175
00:12:53,540 --> 00:12:56,980
Calypso went back to her house, shining among goddesses,

176
00:12:56,980 --> 00:13:00,920
and he set to cutting timbers,
and the work went quickly under him.

177
00:13:00,920 --> 00:13:04,740
Twenty trees in all he felled,
and trimmed them with the bronze,

178
00:13:04,740 --> 00:13:08,620
and dressed them smooth with skill,
and trued them to the line.

179
00:13:08,620 --> 00:13:12,480
Meanwhile Calypso, shining
among goddesses, brought augers,

180
00:13:12,480 --> 00:13:18,480
and he bored through every piece and fitted each to each,
and drove her together with pegs and joinery.

181
00:13:18,480 --> 00:13:24,120
As wide as a man lays out the round of the hull of
a broad cargo ship — a man who knows his craft —

182
00:13:24,120 --> 00:13:27,380
so wide Odysseus built his raft.

183
00:13:27,380 --> 00:13:33,200
He set up half-decks, fitting them to close-set ribs,
and finished with long gunwale planks.

184
00:13:33,200 --> 00:13:39,260
He made a mast in her, and a yard fitted to it,
and he made besides a steering-oar to hold her straight,

185
00:13:39,260 --> 00:13:42,300
and fenced her round from stem
to stern with plaited willow

186
00:13:42,300 --> 00:13:46,120
to break the wave, and heaped down brush for ballast.

187
00:13:46,120 --> 00:13:52,920
Meanwhile Calypso, shining among goddesses, brought
cloth to make a sail; and he made that too, with craft,

188
00:13:52,920 --> 00:13:56,640
and lashed into her the braces,
the halyards, and the sheets.

189
00:13:56,640 --> 00:14:00,080
Then on rollers he hauled her
down to the bright salt water.

190
00:14:00,080 --> 00:14:06,320
It was the fourth day, and all his work was done;
and on the fifth shining Calypso sent him from the island,

191
00:14:06,320 --> 00:14:09,500
having bathed him and dressed him in fragrant clothes.

192
00:14:09,500 --> 00:14:16,240
And the goddess put a skin of dark wine aboard, one skin,
and another, a great one, of water, and provisions

193
00:14:16,240 --> 00:14:23,360
in a sack, and set in plenty of comforting meats;
and she sent up a fair wind for him, harmless and warm.

194
00:14:23,360 --> 00:14:26,720
Rejoicing in the wind, noble Odysseus spread his sail,

195
00:14:26,720 --> 00:14:32,500
and with the steering-oar he steered her, craftsmanlike,
sitting there; and sleep never fell on his eyelids

196
00:14:32,500 --> 00:14:36,260
as he watched the Pleiades, and Boötes that sets late,

197
00:14:36,260 --> 00:14:38,940
and the Bear, that men also call by the name of the Wagon,

198
00:14:38,940 --> 00:14:45,320
who turns in her place and keeps her watch on Orion,
and alone has no share in the baths of Ocean:

199
00:14:45,320 --> 00:14:52,020
for Calypso, shining among goddesses, had told him
to keep her on his left hand as he crossed the sea.

200
00:14:52,020 --> 00:14:57,420
Seventeen days he sailed on, crossing the water,
and on the eighteenth appeared the shadowy mountains

201
00:14:57,420 --> 00:15:00,580
of the Phaeacians' land, where it lay nearest him:

202
00:15:00,580 --> 00:15:02,980
it looked like a shield laid on the misted sea.

203
00:15:02,980 --> 00:15:06,520
But the strong earth-shaker,
coming back from the Ethiopians,

204
00:15:06,520 --> 00:15:09,920
saw him far off, from the
mountains of the Solymi: he showed

205
00:15:09,920 --> 00:15:14,600
to his eye, sailing the sea.
And the god's heart grew the angrier,

206
00:15:14,600 --> 00:15:17,300
and shaking his head he spoke to his own heart:

207
00:15:17,300 --> 00:15:23,120
"Damnation! So the gods have changed their counsels
about Odysseus while I was among the Ethiopians —

208
00:15:23,120 --> 00:15:27,240
and here he is, close to the Phaeacians' land,
where it is his portion

209
00:15:27,240 --> 00:15:30,880
to slip the great noose of misery that has come upon him.

210
00:15:30,880 --> 00:15:34,780
But I say I will yet drive him deep enough in trouble."

211
00:15:34,780 --> 00:15:38,400
So speaking he herded the clouds
together, and stirred up the sea,

212
00:15:38,400 --> 00:15:41,580
the trident in his hands; and he roused all the squalls

213
00:15:41,580 --> 00:15:47,520
of every wind there is, and covered land and sea
alike with cloud; and night rose up out of heaven.

214
00:15:47,520 --> 00:15:51,460
East Wind and South fell on him together,
and the hard-blowing West,

215
00:15:51,460 --> 00:15:55,340
and the North Wind born of the
clear sky, rolling a great wave.

216
00:15:55,340 --> 00:16:01,680
And then Odysseus' knees went loose, and his heart;
and deeply stirred he spoke to his own great heart:

217
00:16:01,680 --> 00:16:04,360
"Ah, wretch that I am — what happens to me at the last?

218
00:16:04,360 --> 00:16:10,240
I fear the goddess spoke it all unerringly,
who said that on the sea, before I reached my own country,

219
00:16:10,240 --> 00:16:14,200
I would fill my measure of pains.
Now it is all coming to pass:

220
00:16:14,200 --> 00:16:17,340
look how Zeus crowns the wide heaven with cloud on cloud,

221
00:16:17,340 --> 00:16:19,940
and has stirred up the sea, and the squalls of every wind

222
00:16:19,940 --> 00:16:23,680
there is come driving.
Now my sheer destruction is certain.

223
00:16:23,680 --> 00:16:27,100
Three times blessed the Danaans, and four times, who died

224
00:16:27,100 --> 00:16:30,780
in those years in wide Troy,
doing the Atreidae's pleasure!

225
00:16:30,780 --> 00:16:35,860
Would that I too had died so, met my doom,
on the day the Trojans in their hundreds hurled

226
00:16:35,860 --> 00:16:39,460
their bronze-tipped spears at
me over the dead son of Peleus.

227
00:16:39,460 --> 00:16:43,320
Then I would have had my death-rites,
and the Achaeans carried my fame;

228
00:16:43,320 --> 00:16:46,360
now it is my lot to be taken in a miserable death."

229
00:16:46,360 --> 00:16:52,360
As he said it, a great wave drove down on him from
above, rushing terribly, and spun the raft around.

230
00:16:52,360 --> 00:16:57,760
Far from the raft he fell, and the steering-oar
flew from his hands; the mast snapped in the middle

231
00:16:57,760 --> 00:17:04,020
under the terrible squall of winds meeting from all
quarters, and sail and yard fell far off in the sea.

232
00:17:04,020 --> 00:17:09,600
And the wave held him under a long time: he could not
rise quickly from beneath the great sea's downrush,

233
00:17:09,600 --> 00:17:13,060
for the clothes weighed on him that shining Calypso gave.

234
00:17:13,060 --> 00:17:16,260
At long last he came up,
spitting from his mouth the bitter

235
00:17:16,260 --> 00:17:19,080
brine that ran streaming in torrents down his head.

236
00:17:19,080 --> 00:17:22,660
Yet even so, ground down as he was,
he did not forget his raft:

237
00:17:22,660 --> 00:17:25,780
he lunged after her through the waves and caught hold,

238
00:17:25,780 --> 00:17:29,080
and sat down in the middle of her,
dodging the end that is death,

239
00:17:29,080 --> 00:17:32,460
while the great wave carried her
with the current, here and there.

240
00:17:32,460 --> 00:17:34,680
As when the North Wind of autumn carries thistledown

241
00:17:34,680 --> 00:17:37,980
across the plain, and the tufts
cling close to one another,

242
00:17:37,980 --> 00:17:41,420
so the winds carried her over
the open water, here and there:

243
00:17:41,420 --> 00:17:44,400
now the South Wind would toss her to the North to carry,

244
00:17:44,400 --> 00:17:48,400
now again the East would yield her to the West to chase.

245
00:17:48,400 --> 00:17:52,540
But the daughter of Cadmus saw him —
Ino of the beautiful ankles,

246
00:17:52,540 --> 00:17:56,180
the White Goddess, who once was
mortal, with a mortal voice,

247
00:17:56,180 --> 00:18:00,140
and now in the salt depths has
her share of honor from the gods.

248
00:18:00,140 --> 00:18:04,060
She pitied Odysseus, driven wandering, worn with pains,

249
00:18:04,060 --> 00:18:08,280
and in the likeness of a shearwater
she rose on the wing from the water,

250
00:18:08,280 --> 00:18:10,940
and perched on the close-bound raft, and said:

251
00:18:10,940 --> 00:18:14,160
"Luckless man, why is Poseidon the earth-shaker

252
00:18:14,160 --> 00:18:18,840
so terribly at odds with you,
that he plants all these evils for you?

253
00:18:18,840 --> 00:18:21,780
Yet he shall not destroy you, for all his fury.

254
00:18:21,780 --> 00:18:25,480
Only do exactly this —
you do not seem to me short of sense.

255
00:18:25,480 --> 00:18:28,260
Strip off these clothes, and leave the raft for the winds

256
00:18:28,260 --> 00:18:31,500
to carry; strike out swimming
with your hands for a homecoming

257
00:18:31,500 --> 00:18:35,180
on the Phaeacians' land,
where it is your portion to escape.

258
00:18:35,180 --> 00:18:37,420
Here — spread this veil under your chest.

259
00:18:37,420 --> 00:18:40,140
It is immortal: no fear then of suffering or death.

260
00:18:40,140 --> 00:18:46,520
But the moment your hands take hold of dry land,
untie it again and throw it into the wine-dark sea,

261
00:18:46,520 --> 00:18:50,140
far out from land, and turn your own face away."

262
00:18:50,140 --> 00:18:55,080
So speaking the goddess gave him the veil,
and she herself sank back into the surging sea

263
00:18:55,080 --> 00:18:59,400
in the likeness of a shearwater,
and the black wave closed over her.

264
00:18:59,400 --> 00:19:05,100
But much-enduring noble Odysseus pondered,
and deeply stirred he spoke to his own great heart:

265
00:19:05,100 --> 00:19:11,800
"Ah me — let this not be some new snare of the immortals
woven against me, when she bids me abandon my raft.

266
00:19:11,800 --> 00:19:17,300
No: I will not obey her yet. Far off with my own
eyes I saw the land where she said my refuge is.

267
00:19:17,300 --> 00:19:20,340
I will do this instead — it seems to me the best:

268
00:19:20,340 --> 00:19:26,280
as long as the timbers hold in their fastenings,
so long I stay aboard and endure my sufferings where I am;

269
00:19:26,280 --> 00:19:31,920
but the moment the wave shakes the raft to pieces,
I swim. There is no better plan to see."

270
00:19:31,920 --> 00:19:35,100
While he was turning this over
in his mind and in his heart,

271
00:19:35,100 --> 00:19:41,380
Poseidon the earth-shaker heaved up a great wave,
terrible and grim, arching over him, and drove it down.

272
00:19:41,380 --> 00:19:46,480
And as a violent wind tosses a heap of dry chaff
and scatters the wisps this way and that way,

273
00:19:46,480 --> 00:19:53,280
so it scattered the raft's long timbers. But Odysseus
got astride one beam, like a man riding a racehorse,

274
00:19:53,280 --> 00:19:58,680
and stripped off the clothes that shining Calypso gave,
and spread the veil at once beneath his chest,

275
00:19:58,680 --> 00:20:05,320
and plunged headfirst in the sea, arms spread wide,
hungry to swim. And the strong earth-shaker saw him,

276
00:20:05,320 --> 00:20:07,680
and shaking his head he spoke to his own heart:

277
00:20:07,680 --> 00:20:13,160
"So — now suffer your many evils and drift the sea,
until you come among a people nurtured of Zeus.

278
00:20:13,160 --> 00:20:16,480
But even so I do not think you
will make light of your trouble."

279
00:20:16,480 --> 00:20:22,460
So speaking he lashed his horses of the beautiful
manes and came to Aegae, where his famous house is.

280
00:20:22,460 --> 00:20:26,280
But Athena, daughter of Zeus, thought of other things.

281
00:20:26,280 --> 00:20:30,880
She bound up the courses of all the other winds
and commanded every one to rest and fall asleep,

282
00:20:30,880 --> 00:20:35,500
and roused the quick North Wind,
and broke the waves before him,

283
00:20:35,500 --> 00:20:39,480
so that Zeus-born Odysseus might come among the oar-loving

284
00:20:39,480 --> 00:20:42,100
Phaeacians, having slipped past death and the dooms.

285
00:20:42,100 --> 00:20:45,020
Two nights then and two days on the close-packed swell

286
00:20:45,020 --> 00:20:49,200
he drifted, and many times his heart
looked destruction in the face.

287
00:20:49,200 --> 00:20:52,580
But when Dawn of the lovely braids
brought the third day round,

288
00:20:52,580 --> 00:20:58,800
then at last the wind fell, and a windless
calm came on; and he saw the land close by,

289
00:20:58,800 --> 00:21:02,180
casting a sharp glance forward, lifted on a great wave.

290
00:21:02,180 --> 00:21:05,560
As when a father's life shows
dear and welcome to his children —

291
00:21:05,560 --> 00:21:12,260
a father who lies in sickness suffering strong pains,
long wasting away, some hateful power upon him,

292
00:21:12,260 --> 00:21:16,040
and then, dear and welcome,
the gods set him free of his evil —

293
00:21:16,040 --> 00:21:19,060
so welcome to Odysseus showed the land and the trees,

294
00:21:19,060 --> 00:21:23,040
and he swam hard, straining to set foot on solid earth.

295
00:21:23,040 --> 00:21:29,540
But when he was as far offshore as a man's shout carries,
he heard the boom of sea against the rock-teeth:

296
00:21:29,540 --> 00:21:32,520
the great swell thundered on the dry land's edge,

297
00:21:32,520 --> 00:21:36,240
belching terribly, and everything
was shrouded in salt spray.

298
00:21:36,240 --> 00:21:40,060
For there were no harbors there
to hold ships, no roadsteads —

299
00:21:40,060 --> 00:21:43,780
only jutting headlands, rock-teeth, and cliff-faces.

300
00:21:43,780 --> 00:21:50,040
And then Odysseus' knees went loose, and his heart,
and deeply stirred he spoke to his own great heart:

301
00:21:50,040 --> 00:21:56,380
"Ah me — now that Zeus has given me sight of land
unhoped for, and I have won across this gulf at last,

302
00:21:56,380 --> 00:21:59,260
no way out of the gray salt water shows anywhere:

303
00:21:59,260 --> 00:22:05,300
sharp crags offshore, and all around them the wave
roaring and churning, and the rock running up sheer,

304
00:22:05,300 --> 00:22:10,200
and the sea deep close in — nowhere to
stand on both my feet and escape the evil.

305
00:22:10,200 --> 00:22:12,740
If I try to climb out, the great wave may snatch me

306
00:22:12,740 --> 00:22:16,160
and hurl me on the standing stone,
and my try go for nothing.

307
00:22:16,160 --> 00:22:22,140
But if I swim along still farther, hoping to find
shelving beaches somewhere, and harbors of the sea,

308
00:22:22,140 --> 00:22:28,240
I fear the storm-squall snatches me up again and carries me,
groaning heavily, over the fish-filled sea —

309
00:22:28,240 --> 00:22:34,600
or some power looses a monster on me out of the brine,
one of the swarm that famous Amphitrite pastures:

310
00:22:34,600 --> 00:22:37,720
for I know how the famous shaker
of earth is at odds with me."

311
00:22:37,720 --> 00:22:43,480
While he was turning this over in his mind and in his heart,
a great wave carried him onto the jagged shore.

312
00:22:43,480 --> 00:22:46,940
There his skin would have been
stripped, his bones smashed in,

313
00:22:46,940 --> 00:22:50,200
had not the goddess, gray-eyed
Athena, put a thought in him:

314
00:22:50,200 --> 00:22:55,980
lunging in, he seized the rock with both hands and
clung there groaning while the great wave went past.

315
00:22:55,980 --> 00:23:02,260
That one he escaped so; but its backwash leapt on him
again and struck him and threw him far out to sea.

316
00:23:02,260 --> 00:23:07,160
As when an octopus is dragged out of its den
and thick pebbles cling in the cups of its arms,

317
00:23:07,160 --> 00:23:14,020
so from his bold hands the skin was stripped away
against the rocks; and the great wave closed over him.

318
00:23:14,020 --> 00:23:17,400
There, before his time,
wretched Odysseus would have perished,

319
00:23:17,400 --> 00:23:20,620
had not gray-eyed Athena given him clear sense.

320
00:23:20,620 --> 00:23:23,560
Coming up out of the surf where it belches at the land,

321
00:23:23,560 --> 00:23:27,120
he swam along outside it,
looking to the shore in hope of finding

322
00:23:27,120 --> 00:23:30,380
shelving beaches somewhere, and harbors of the sea.

323
00:23:30,380 --> 00:23:33,740
But when, swimming, he came to
the mouth of a fair-flowing river,

324
00:23:33,740 --> 00:23:39,320
there at last the ground seemed best to him,
clear of stones, and there was shelter from the wind.

325
00:23:39,320 --> 00:23:42,300
He felt the current flowing and prayed in his heart:

326
00:23:42,300 --> 00:23:46,860
"Hear me, lord, whoever you are.
I come to you — the much-prayed-to —

327
00:23:46,860 --> 00:23:50,000
a fugitive out of the sea, from the threats of Poseidon.

328
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:54,980
Even to the deathless gods that man is
sacred who comes as a wanderer, as I come now

329
00:23:54,980 --> 00:23:58,180
to your stream and to your knees, after much labor.

330
00:23:58,180 --> 00:24:01,220
Pity me, lord: I declare myself your suppliant."

331
00:24:01,220 --> 00:24:06,020
And at once the god stayed his
current, held back the wave,

332
00:24:06,020 --> 00:24:12,420
and made the water calm before him, and brought him
safe into the river's mouth. And he bent both knees

333
00:24:12,420 --> 00:24:17,440
and let his strong hands fall:
the sea had crushed the heart in him.

334
00:24:17,440 --> 00:24:21,040
All his flesh was swollen, and the sea oozed in streams

335
00:24:21,040 --> 00:24:24,780
up through his mouth and nostrils.
Breathless and speechless

336
00:24:24,780 --> 00:24:28,980
he lay, barely alive,
and a terrible weariness came over him.

337
00:24:28,980 --> 00:24:33,040
But when he got his breath again,
and the spirit gathered in his chest,

338
00:24:33,040 --> 00:24:38,680
then he unbound the goddess's veil from his body
and let it go into the river running seaward,

339
00:24:38,680 --> 00:24:42,280
and the great wave carried it back
down the current, and quickly Ino

340
00:24:42,280 --> 00:24:46,280
took it in her own dear hands.
And he turned from the river,

341
00:24:46,280 --> 00:24:49,500
sank down in the reeds, and kissed the grain-giving earth;

342
00:24:49,500 --> 00:24:52,840
and deeply stirred he spoke to his own great heart:

343
00:24:52,840 --> 00:24:56,360
"Ah me, what now? What happens to me at the last?

344
00:24:56,360 --> 00:24:59,540
If I keep watch through the
anxious night here by the river,

345
00:24:59,540 --> 00:25:06,380
the two together — the evil frost and the soaking dew —
may finish me: weak as I am, my spirit gasping;

346
00:25:06,380 --> 00:25:09,480
and toward dawn a cold breeze blows off a river.

347
00:25:09,480 --> 00:25:14,740
But if I climb the slope to the shadowy wood and
fall asleep in the thick brush — supposing cold

348
00:25:14,740 --> 00:25:21,540
and weariness release me, and sweet sleep comes —
I fear I become spoil and carrion for wild beasts."

349
00:25:21,540 --> 00:25:24,060
Yet as he weighed it, this seemed the better way:

350
00:25:24,060 --> 00:25:26,800
he went up toward the wood, and found it near the water

351
00:25:26,800 --> 00:25:30,120
on a rise in the open. He crept in under a double thicket

352
00:25:30,120 --> 00:25:34,800
grown from a single stock —
one wild olive, one the planted tree.

353
00:25:34,800 --> 00:25:37,800
Through these no strength of
wet-blowing winds could pierce,

354
00:25:37,800 --> 00:25:43,820
nor could the shining sun strike through with its rays,
nor rain soak through them, so close they grew,

355
00:25:43,820 --> 00:25:49,060
laced into one another. Under these Odysseus
crept, and heaped himself at once a wide bed

356
00:25:49,060 --> 00:25:55,020
with his own hands: for there was a drift of fallen
leaves there, deep, enough to shelter two men or three

357
00:25:55,020 --> 00:25:58,260
in the winter season, however hard it blew.

358
00:25:58,260 --> 00:26:01,580
Seeing it, much-enduring noble Odysseus rejoiced,

359
00:26:01,580 --> 00:26:05,560
and he lay down in the middle and
poured the fallen leaves over him.

360
00:26:05,560 --> 00:26:08,480
As when a man buries a burning log in a black ash-heap

361
00:26:08,480 --> 00:26:12,520
at the far edge of the fields —
a man with no neighbors near —

362
00:26:12,520 --> 00:26:16,120
saving the seed of fire,
so as not to seek a light elsewhere,

363
00:26:16,120 --> 00:26:22,140
so Odysseus buried himself in the leaves.
And Athena poured sleep upon his eyes, to free him soonest

364
00:26:22,140 --> 00:26:26,426
from his toil-worn weariness,
when she had folded his dear eyelids shut.
